            <ArticleTitle>Arachnoid granulations: a rare cause of lytic occipital bone lesion.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>603-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Arachnoid granulation is often found incidentally in the dural sinuses and skull. It may also enlarge the dural sinus or inner table of the skull. We report a 46-year-old woman who presented with occipital headaches and arachnoid granulations in both transverse sinuses and torcular herophili. Neurological examination was normal. Fundoscopic examination, visual fields and acuity were normal. The headache resolved with medical treatment. No intervention for these lesions was planned. The patient was followed up with magnetic resonance imaging studies.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
